INT. STARLITE LANES - BOWLING ALLEY - NIGHT

The neon is half dead. Lane nine glows pink. A disco ball turns slowly over the shoe counter, throwing coins of light across a carpet printed with comets. It smells of fryer oil and rental-shoe disinfectant. The last night of high school, and the party has thinned to six.

PHOEBE ADEBAYO, 18, still in her cap and gown, kneels on lane six and rolls the ball down the gutter on purpose. KENJI MORIKAWA, 18, tie around his forehead, sits on the ball return and watches her. LACHLAN "LOCKY" DUNMORE, 18, hair gelled for an event that has ended, flips a keychain in one hand. BEATRIX SOKOLOWSKI, 17, writes in a notebook with her tongue between her teeth. COSMO ABARA, 18, who sold the most tickets to the dance and attended none of it, eats fries from a paper boat.

HORACE LINDQVIST, 60s, the night manager, leans on the counter with a rag over his shoulder and counts a cash drawer.

HORACE
Last frame, kids. I'm closing.

PHOEBE
Five more minutes, Mr. Lindqvist.

HORACE
That's what you said at the last five.

KENJI
Mr. Lindqvist, this is history.

HORACE
It's a Tuesday.

BEATRIX
(not looking up)
It's a Tuesday in history.

Locky tosses his keys. Cosmo catches them in a fry. A beat. Everyone looks at the fry-wrapped keys.

COSMO
I regret nothing.

LOCKY
Those are my dad's keys.

COSMO
Then I regret one thing.

Phoebe lines up the ball again. Kenji slides off the return. Beatrix closes the notebook.

BEATRIX
Okay. Real talk.

PHOEBE
Oh no.

BEATRIX
Does anyone actually know what they're doing in September?

KENJI
Michigan.

LOCKY
Shop. My uncle's.

COSMO
Community college. Then, like, the world.

PHOEBE
Nothing.

They look at her.

PHOEBE (CONT'D)
I don't know. Nothing's planned. That's the plan.

The disco ball turns. Light slides across their faces, one by one.

HORACE
(calling)
Last call. Or I turn off lane six.

Phoebe takes a breath. Rolls the ball. It wobbles, wobbles, and drifts toward the pins.

Nobody breathes.

It hits the head pin, perfect. Pins explode.

STRIKE flashes on the screen in dying neon.

They don't cheer. They stand there, grinning, like people who have just remembered how.

PHOEBE (CONT'D)
(quiet)
Huh.
